1
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:12,367
I came to Thorpe Lodge on the eighth of November, 1911, in rain,

2
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:16,960
with one trunk and a lantern I had bought in Leeds for the purpose.

3
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:21,000
The carrier set me down at the gate and would go no further.

4
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:22,860
He did not say why.

5
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:26,372
He took his money and turned the cart in the lane,

6
00:00:26,372 --> 00:00:30,940
and I waited with the trunk at my feet and looked at what I had been hired to save.

7
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:35,280
You will want to know what it looked like, so I shall tell you plainly.

8
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,400
Three ranges of grey stone were set round a cloister garth gone to bracken.

9
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:42,000
The roofs were gone.

10
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:46,852
The windows were open to the sky, and the rain went straight through them onto the

11
00:00:46,852 --> 00:00:47,452
floors.

12
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,730
There had been a fire in 1811, and in the hundred years since,

13
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,400
nobody had touched the place.

14
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:56,720
A woman was waiting under the gatehouse arch.

15
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:00,186
She was stout and past sixty, in a man's oilskin,

16
00:01:00,186 --> 00:01:03,440
and she held a ring of keys against her chest.

17
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,880
"Miss Ashworth?" she said.

18
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:07,260
"Yes.

19
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:08,500
Mrs Orme?"

20
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:09,760
"Brenda Orme.

21
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,640
I keep the gate lodge for the Estate, though I sleep in the village.

22
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:14,980
She did not offer her hand.

23
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:16,300
She offered the keys.

24
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:17,760
"Eleven there.

25
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:20,140
You'll want the brass one for the muniment room.

26
00:01:20,540 --> 00:01:21,860
The rest you'll find out."

27
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:23,680
I took the ring.

28
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:25,540
It was heavier than it looked.

29
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:28,840
"Has anyone else been in since the fire?

30
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:29,440
I asked.

31
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:30,560
"One man.

32
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:33,625
She turned and walked ahead of me into the garth,

33
00:01:33,625 --> 00:01:36,820
and I followed with the trunk dragging behind me on its strap.

34
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,040
"A surveyor, in 'twenty-one.

35
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:42,280
Eighteen twenty-one, ten years after it burned.

36
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:44,800
He was sent to measure the place for the Estate.

37
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:45,780
Same as you."

38
00:01:46,420 --> 00:01:47,020
"And?"

39
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,380
"He locked himself in the east range.

40
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:51,200
She pointed with her chin.

41
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:54,400
It was the longest of the three, and the best preserved.

42
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,280
"Barred the door from inside.

43
00:01:56,900 --> 00:01:58,680
He shouted through it for two days.

44
00:01:59,140 --> 00:01:59,740
Then he stopped."

45
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:01,360
"Shouted what?"

46
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:03,200
"That he'd not open it.

47
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:04,740
That it wasn't to be named.

48
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:06,740
She looked at me at last.

49
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:09,340
Her eyes were pale and quite steady.

50
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:11,840
"They broke the door on the third day.

51
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:14,480
I'm told he'd gone, and the room was empty.

52
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:16,580
But I'm only telling you what's said."

53
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:18,540
I thanked her.

54
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:20,420
I believe I smiled.

55
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:24,789
I was thirty-four, and I had worked in ruins for eleven years,

56
00:02:24,789 --> 00:02:27,900
and I thought I knew what the villages made of them.

57
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:31,880
She left me at the foot of the night stair with the rain easing.

58
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:33,640
I lit the lantern.

59
00:02:34,660 --> 00:02:38,060
I began that evening with the west range, because it was safest.

60
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,240
I paced the dormitory floor and wrote figures in my notebook.

61
00:02:43,360 --> 00:02:46,200
Near ten o'clock I came to a chamber off the passage.

62
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:49,294
Its doorway had been bricked up long ago,

63
00:02:49,294 --> 00:02:51,940
and a narrow window was cut high in the blocking.

64
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:54,380
I held the lantern to it.

65
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:57,020
On the sill lay a stub of candle.

66
00:02:57,940 --> 00:02:59,240
I did not touch it at first.

67
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,260
I looked, and then I put out one finger.

68
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:04,760
The wax gave under the pressure.

69
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,660
It was still faintly warm, and it smelled of tallow.

70
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:11,220
I did not move for a good while.

71
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:14,220
Then I told myself that it was mine.

72
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:17,480
I had carried a candle in my coat since Leeds.

73
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:21,120
"It's mine," I said aloud, to the passage.

74
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:23,500
"I set it down and forgot it.

75
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:24,520
That's all."

76
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:30,860
I wrote wax, west range, ten o'clock in my notebook, and I did not check my coat.

77
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:36,055
I took the cloister next, and I took it slowly,

78
00:03:36,055 --> 00:03:38,860
because I wanted to be tired by morning.

79
00:03:39,840 --> 00:03:42,020
The north walk measured sixty-one feet.

80
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:46,240
The south walk measured sixty feet eleven.

81
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:49,120
That was the fire, or the settling after it.

82
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:52,780
"Sixty-one," I said, as I wrote it down.

83
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,920
"Sixty, eleven.

84
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:58,380
I have always called my figures aloud.

85
00:03:58,380 --> 00:03:59,560
It keeps the hand honest.

86
00:04:00,520 --> 00:04:04,494
The lantern hung from a nail I had driven into the crumbling mortar,

87
00:04:04,494 --> 00:04:07,300
and every few minutes I moved it along the wall.

88
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:10,920
On the third night I found the second candle.

89
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:14,610
It was in a bricked doorway off the chapter house,

90
00:04:14,610 --> 00:04:17,160
again on a sill cut high in the blocking.

91
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:21,600
A stub, burned down to a shallow puddle in its own grease.

92
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:23,400
I touched it.

93
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:24,860
Warm.

94
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:27,560
Warm as a cup that has just been put down.

95
00:04:28,460 --> 00:04:30,820
I did not say anything to the passage this time.

96
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:34,040
On the fourth night there were two more.

97
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:39,120
On the fifth, one in the infirmary passage, and one beneath the dorter stair.

98
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:45,340
Each was a single candle, and each had burned out, and each was soft.

99
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:47,520
It was all quite plain.

100
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:49,340
No smoke on the stone.

101
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,760
No footprint in the ash on the floor.

102
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:53,660
No wick left standing.

103
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:58,225
Only the wax, and the warmth in it, and the smell of tallow,

104
00:04:58,225 --> 00:05:01,300
which by then I knew before I reached the door.

105
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:04,860
I began to arrive at those sills before I meant to.

106
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:06,840
My feet had learned the way.

107
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:09,880
On the fifth night I checked my coat.

108
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:15,236
I turned out every pocket onto the cloister wall: a pencil, a tape,

109
00:05:15,236 --> 00:05:17,480
the ring of keys, a handkerchief.

110
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:19,360
There was no candle.

111
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:21,160
There had never been one.

112
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,120
"Well," I said to the empty walk.

113
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:25,580
"There it is."

114
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:29,260
I put the lining back and buttoned the coat to the neck.

115
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:32,940
On the sixth day I went looking for paper.

116
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:37,420
Anyone restoring a building ought to know what was drawn before the fire.

117
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:41,339
My letter of commission, signed in Mr Brindle's name,

118
00:05:41,339 --> 00:05:43,460
had said the muniment room was sound.

119
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:47,042
It lay behind the library, up eleven steps,

120
00:05:47,042 --> 00:05:49,600
with an oak door that stuck in its frame.

121
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:51,780
I put my shoulder to it.

122
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:58,300
It gave all at once, and I was inside, in a room that smelled of mice and old glue.

123
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:00,520
The presses were full.

124
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:05,460
Most of it was leases and tithe accounts, tied in tape gone brown.

125
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:11,700
But on the lowest shelf, wrapped in oilcloth, I found a roll of plans on linen.

126
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:16,060
I carried them to the window and unrolled them across the sill.

127
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,620
I weighted the corners with my notebook, my pencil, and two stones from the floor.

128
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:24,580
The hand was small and upright.

129
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:26,380
A surveyor's hand.

130
00:06:27,060 --> 00:06:33,880
In the corner were a date, 1821, and an initial, and the word Thorpe.

131
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:36,920
I knew the west range at once.

132
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:39,920
I knew the cloister, with its north walk.

133
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:45,580
Then my eye went to the east range, and I went on looking for a long time.

134
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:49,740
Beneath it, drawn in finer ink, was a stair.

135
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:51,580
It turned twice.

136
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:55,521
At the foot of it lay a chapel, square and low,

137
00:06:55,521 --> 00:07:00,700
with an altar on the north wall and a single narrow desk set against the south.

138
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:04,340
None of the maps sent with my commission showed it.

139
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:09,080
In the margin, in the same upright hand, were five words.

140
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,340
They were pressed harder than the rest, so hard that the nib had split.

141
00:07:15,600 --> 00:07:17,560
It counts those who measure.

142
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:19,840
I read them aloud.

143
00:07:20,580 --> 00:07:21,300
I don't know why.

144
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:24,400
My voice came back thin off the plaster.

145
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:29,600
Then I turned the plan over, as one does, to look for a note on the back.

146
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:35,680
And there, in pencil, fresh and without a smudge, someone had added a tally.

147
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:37,380
Six strokes.

148
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:38,780
A gap.

149
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:41,100
And a seventh, not yet finished.

150
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:46,600
Brenda Orme came to the cloister at dusk that evening,

151
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:48,765
as she had every evening that week,

152
00:07:48,765 --> 00:07:51,920
with a loaf under a cloth and her lantern swinging.

153
00:07:52,720 --> 00:07:55,120
I met her by the well-head.

154
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:59,500
I had the plans rolled under my arm, and I had not yet decided to hide them.

155
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:01,360
She saw them anyway.

156
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,720
She looked at the oilcloth, and then at me.

157
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:07,880
"You've been in the muniment room," she said.

158
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:10,020
"I've been where the work is."

159
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:13,560
"Leave the east range alone, Miss Ashworth.

160
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:16,180
She set the bread on the well-head.

161
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,460
Her hands were quite steady.

162
00:08:18,420 --> 00:08:19,220
"Measure the west.

163
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:22,660
Measure the cloister, the church, the gatehouse.

164
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:26,760
Mr Brindle can have his report, and you can go home to Leeds."

165
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:28,100
"Why?"

166
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:30,900
"Because he was my great-grandfather."

167
00:08:31,780 --> 00:08:33,280
I did not say anything.

168
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:35,600
The light was going from the stones.

169
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:38,320
"The surveyor," Brenda said.

170
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:41,880
"That's his initial in the corner of those plans, if you'd looked.

171
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:44,020
We don't say his name up here.

172
00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:48,150
He went in there with his instruments in the autumn of 'twenty-one,

173
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:49,880
and he turned the key from inside.

174
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:51,880
My grandmother was nine.

175
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:55,680
She knelt at that door three days and spoke to it."

176
00:08:56,460 --> 00:08:57,240
"And did it answer?"

177
00:08:58,100 --> 00:08:58,860
"Something did.

178
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:01,180
She pulled her shawl tighter.

179
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,220
"My family has kept the keys ever since.

180
00:09:04,860 --> 00:09:05,720
Every one of us.

181
00:09:06,260 --> 00:09:07,000
Not for the money.

182
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:11,220
The Estate pays us eleven pounds a year, and it wouldn't keep a hen.

183
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:14,720
We keep them so that nobody finishes his drawings."

184
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:17,360
I should tell you that I laughed.

185
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:21,820
It was a short, ugly sound, and I was ashamed of it at once.

186
00:09:21,820 --> 00:09:24,680
"They're only drawings," I said.

187
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:26,720
"Then you won't mind leaving them."

188
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:28,880
I said I would think about it.

189
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:31,700
Brenda looked at me for a long moment.

190
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:34,309
She nodded as one nods to the sick,

191
00:09:34,309 --> 00:09:37,180
and went out through the gate without her lantern.

192
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:40,780
I listened to her boots until the lane took them.

193
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:44,100
Then I did what I had always done when I was afraid.

194
00:09:44,760 --> 00:09:45,360
I measured.

195
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:49,573
I unrolled the plans on the well-head beside her loaf,

196
00:09:49,573 --> 00:09:52,840
and set her lantern on one corner to hold them flat.

197
00:09:53,540 --> 00:09:58,020
Then I took the steel tape and the plumb line along the outside of the east range.

198
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,420
I did not open any door.

199
00:10:01,220 --> 00:10:05,920
I paced the walk, and counted the bays, and laid the tape along the foundations.

200
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:11,220
After each figure I went back to the well-head and checked it against his.

201
00:10:12,180 --> 00:10:12,780
They agreed.

202
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:16,560
Sixty-one feet on the north walk, as I had found.

203
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:21,020
Eleven feet four inches from the south corner to the first buttress.

204
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:25,280
The wall at the chapter-house doorway was nineteen inches thick.

205
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:29,000
I had taken that at noon and written it down in pencil.

206
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:32,360
His ink said nineteen.

207
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,140
"A good surveyor measures well," I said aloud.

208
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:36,680
"That's all it proves."

209
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:41,280
But the east wall, by my tape, ran forty-four feet.

210
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:47,487
On his plan the same wall ran forty-four feet, with a blank stretch in the middle,

211
00:10:47,487 --> 00:10:48,440
nine feet wide.

212
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:52,580
My tape had passed over that stretch and found solid brick.

213
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:55,180
On his plan it was a room.

214
00:10:56,020 --> 00:10:56,980
I walked it again.

215
00:10:57,780 --> 00:10:59,100
The tape said brick.

216
00:10:59,960 --> 00:11:03,400
His ink said a door, with a hinge drawn on the left side.

217
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:07,580
I waited a long while with the tape slack in my hand.

218
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:11,380
Then I went to the brick and laid my palm flat on it.

219
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:12,900
It was warm.

220
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:19,365
At a quarter to midnight I was still at the east wall, alone,

221
00:11:19,365 --> 00:11:21,260
with my palm on the brick.

222
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:24,620
It had given something back all that time.

223
00:11:25,640 --> 00:11:30,640
Warmth, steady and low, like a loaf drawn from an oven an hour ago.

224
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:33,900
I should tell you that I did not think of leaving.

225
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:36,700
I thought of the lump hammer in my trunk.

226
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:39,980
I fetched it, and a cold chisel with it.

227
00:11:40,740 --> 00:11:43,120
I read his ink once more by the lantern.

228
00:11:44,040 --> 00:11:45,780
"Hinge side," I said.

229
00:11:46,380 --> 00:11:47,140
"Then we'll see."

230
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:51,100
It took me until the church clock in the village struck two.

231
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:55,480
The mortar was older than the fire, and soft with lime.

232
00:11:56,480 --> 00:12:01,349
Courses came away in my hands, and I stacked them against the cloister wall, neatly,

233
00:12:01,349 --> 00:12:03,320
as I would have done for a client.

234
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:07,260
Behind the ninth course the lantern flame leaned.

235
00:12:08,080 --> 00:12:11,440
Air was moving out of the hole, and it smelled of tallow.

236
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:14,860
I widened the gap until I could pass through it.

237
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:16,800
Then I went in.

238
00:12:18,080 --> 00:12:19,260
A stair went down.

239
00:12:19,260 --> 00:12:23,320
Twelve treads, by my count, each worn hollow in the middle.

240
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:26,820
I laid the tape along the first of them out of habit.

241
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:28,260
Nine inches.

242
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:30,060
His plan said nine.

243
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,540
At the bottom lay the chapel.

244
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:35,600
It was low, and the vault had not fallen.

245
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,540
Nothing in it was burned.

246
00:12:38,280 --> 00:12:41,020
The stalls were whole, and the floor had been swept.

247
00:12:41,740 --> 00:12:43,880
I looked for dust, and found none.

248
00:12:44,840 --> 00:12:46,940
"Somebody keeps this," I said.

249
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:49,080
My voice did not echo.

250
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:51,720
The room took it and kept it.

251
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:56,866
At the far end was a narrow desk, the sort a draughtsman uses standing,

252
00:12:56,866 --> 00:12:59,220
with a tall stool pushed in beneath it.

253
00:12:59,960 --> 00:13:01,520
A drawing was pinned to the desk.

254
00:13:02,100 --> 00:13:04,960
A pen lay across the paper with the nib still wet.

255
00:13:05,660 --> 00:13:08,580
Beside it, a candle burned in a pewter dish.

256
00:13:09,260 --> 00:13:12,000
The wax at its foot had not yet begun to pool.

257
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:13,560
I went to it.

258
00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:15,220
I did not decide to.

259
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:19,900
The drawing showed the east range in section, and it was good work.

260
00:13:20,680 --> 00:13:22,380
The cloister bays were drawn true.

261
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:25,072
The stair was there, and the chapel,

262
00:13:25,072 --> 00:13:28,740
and a doorway in the chapel's south wall that I had not yet seen.

263
00:13:29,460 --> 00:13:30,780
The lettering was mine.

264
00:13:30,780 --> 00:13:33,895
I know my own sevens, with the bar through them,

265
00:13:33,895 --> 00:13:36,880
and my habit of underlining a dimension twice.

266
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:42,060
In the box where I put the date, I read the fifteenth of November.

267
00:13:43,140 --> 00:13:44,280
I looked at my watch.

268
00:13:45,120 --> 00:13:49,300
It was ten minutes past two, on the morning of the fourteenth.

269
00:13:49,300 --> 00:13:50,860
Then I heard the telephone.

270
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:53,200
Thorpe Lodge had none.

271
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,275
The Estate had promised a line, and the only instrument for four miles was at the

272
00:13:58,275 --> 00:13:59,700
post office in the village.

273
00:14:00,420 --> 00:14:03,700
But above me, in the cloister, something rang.

274
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:07,900
A bell on a brass wire, thin and insistent.

275
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:12,920
I climbed the twelve treads and followed it across the yard to the gate lodge.

276
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:17,020
A lamp was lit in the kitchen window, though no one was there.

277
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,360
A black receiver lay on the kitchen table, off its hook,

278
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:23,220
and a voice came small from it.

279
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:24,760
I picked it up.

280
00:14:25,380 --> 00:14:26,280
"Miss Ashworth?

281
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:27,360
Is that you?

282
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:30,100
It's Gerald Brindle, at the Estate office.

283
00:14:30,720 --> 00:14:32,420
I've been trying the inn since nine."

284
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:34,940
"Mr Brindle," I said.

285
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:36,460
"It's two in the morning."

286
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:38,100
"Yes, I know.

287
00:14:38,660 --> 00:14:39,260
Forgive me.

288
00:14:39,540 --> 00:14:41,420
I only learned this evening.

289
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:42,380
He cleared his throat.

290
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:46,300
"Miss Ashworth, I must ask where you got the commission.

291
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,080
We never offered it to anyone of that name.

292
00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:51,800
We have sent no surveyor to Thorpe Lodge.

293
00:14:52,260 --> 00:14:54,020
Not this year, nor in my time."

294
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:56,920
I held the receiver against my ear.

295
00:14:57,700 --> 00:15:00,340
Across the yard I could see the gap I had opened.

296
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:02,240
A light was burning in it.

297
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:04,700
"Mr Brindle," I said.

298
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:06,680
"Then who has been paying me?"

299
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:12,440
I was still in the gate lodge kitchen, with the receiver at my ear.

300
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:14,880
"Then who has been paying me?

301
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:17,720
I said again, because he had not answered.

302
00:15:18,460 --> 00:15:20,420
The line gave me only a crackle.

303
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:24,449
Then Mr Brindle said, very gently, "Miss Ashworth,

304
00:15:24,449 --> 00:15:27,740
I think you should come away from there tonight.

305
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:30,080
I'll send a motor at first light."

306
00:15:30,840 --> 00:15:32,180
"Yes," I said.

307
00:15:32,800 --> 00:15:33,400
"Do."

308
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:39,030
I put the receiver down on the table, and it went on speaking, small and faithful,

309
00:15:39,030 --> 00:15:40,200
to the empty room.

310
00:15:40,920 --> 00:15:42,360
I did not stay to hear it.

311
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:43,840
I crossed the yard.

312
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:49,198
At the well-head I took the ring of keys from my coat and laid it beside the lantern

313
00:15:49,198 --> 00:15:51,520
Brenda had left, where she would find it.

314
00:15:51,940 --> 00:15:54,120
I did not need keys where I was going.

315
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:57,160
The light in the gap did not move as I came.

316
00:15:57,900 --> 00:15:58,700
I was not afraid.

317
00:15:59,420 --> 00:16:00,580
I had expected to be.

318
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:04,452
I went down the twelve treads and through the chapel,

319
00:16:04,452 --> 00:16:08,540
and the candle was burning on the narrow desk exactly as I had left it.

320
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:10,680
The pen lay across the paper.

321
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:15,940
My drawing waited under it, the east range in section, every wall true.

322
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,100
One wall was missing.

323
00:16:18,760 --> 00:16:19,740
I saw that at once.

324
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:24,140
A short wall, at the foot of the stair, closing the last gap.

325
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:25,840
I knew where it went.

326
00:16:26,520 --> 00:16:31,400
I knew the length of it as I knew my own name, eleven feet and four inches.

327
00:16:32,120 --> 00:16:35,680
I knew I would underline the figure twice, as I always did.

328
00:16:36,460 --> 00:16:38,240
You will say I might have walked out.

329
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:43,184
I thought of the motor coming at first light, and Mr Brindle's kind voice,

330
00:16:43,184 --> 00:16:45,660
and the commission that no one had given me.

331
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:48,660
Then I thought of the drawing, left as it was.

332
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:51,380
I drew out the stool and sat.

333
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:54,720
It took my weight as though it had been waiting for it.

334
00:16:54,720 --> 00:17:00,280
"Eleven feet four," I said aloud, and my voice was small in the vault.

335
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:02,080
I took up the pen.

336
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:04,880
The nib was dry, and then it was not.

337
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:10,490
I set the rule against the paper and drew the line from left to right, slowly,

338
00:17:10,490 --> 00:17:13,060
and the wall appeared where it had to be.

339
00:17:13,860 --> 00:17:17,780
Behind me, a long way up, stone moved against stone.

340
00:17:18,520 --> 00:17:20,560
It was a soft sound, and patient.

341
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:22,900
A brick, and then another.

342
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:24,440
I did not turn.

343
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:26,880
I underlined the dimension twice.

344
00:17:27,620 --> 00:17:29,660
The candle burned a little straighter.

345
00:17:30,520 --> 00:17:33,973
Its flame stopped leaning and rose tall and still,

346
00:17:33,973 --> 00:17:37,840
and the wax beneath it ran warm and clear down the side.

347
00:17:38,880 --> 00:17:43,500
At dawn Brenda Orme came across the yard with a fresh loaf in her apron.

348
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:48,740
The gate lodge door was open, and the black receiver lay on its side on the table.

349
00:17:49,540 --> 00:17:50,540
She did not go in.

350
00:17:51,260 --> 00:17:55,666
She walked on to the east range, to the place where the wall had been broken through

351
00:17:55,666 --> 00:17:57,240
the night before, and stopped.

352
00:17:58,120 --> 00:17:59,320
The wall was whole.

353
00:18:00,220 --> 00:18:03,040
The brick was sound and the mortar dark with age.

354
00:18:03,960 --> 00:18:06,307
The courses had been laid from the far side,

355
00:18:06,307 --> 00:18:08,440
for the trowel marks all pointed inward.

356
00:18:09,260 --> 00:18:11,340
She put her palm flat against it.

357
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:13,980
It was dry, and quite cold.

358
00:18:14,980 --> 00:18:19,040
On the ledge of the first buttress was a stub of candle, newly burned.

359
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:22,100
When she touched it, the wax was warm.

360
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:26,880
She went back to the well-head, where the ring of keys lay beside her lantern.

361
00:18:27,740 --> 00:18:28,540
She picked it up.

362
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:33,100
She counted the keys once, and then again, with her lips moving.

363
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:35,640
There had always been eleven.

364
00:18:36,360 --> 00:18:41,760
She held up the last one, new and bright, with no rust on it and no tarnish.

365
00:18:42,620 --> 00:18:47,120
A paper tag was tied through the bow, in a small, upright hand she knew.

366
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:49,400
It read, Ashworth.

367
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:52,240
Brenda Orme put the ring in her apron.

368
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:56,840
Then she went back to the wall, and set the loaf on the ledge beside the wax.

